# Blueprint Chart (`.bpc`) authoring

When the user wants a chart, graph, or data visualization, author it as a Blueprint Chart
`.bpc` document. It is a plain-text DSL that renders to an interactive, accessible SVG with no
backend and no account.

## Loop

1. Pick a chart type for the data shape.
2. Write the `.bpc`.
3. Validate before presenting (Blueprint Chart MCP `validate_dsl`; fix every error/warning).
4. Render (MCP `render`, or open at https://blueprintchart.com).

**Never invent property names, chart types, or values.** If unsure a field exists, call
`get_grammar` / `get_example` (MCP) or check https://docs.blueprintchart.com. An invented field
is a parse error, not a feature.

## Shape

One `chart` block: chart type, properties, a `data` block, optional `highlight` / `annotation`,
optional `scene` blocks.

- **Chart types:** `line`, `line-multi`, `bar-vertical`, `bar-horizontal`, `bar-grouped`,
  `bar-stacked`, `bar-multi`, `bar-split`, `column-stacked`, `area`, `area-stacked`, `pie`,
  `donut`, `waterfall`.
- **Data:** single series `"label" = value` per line; multi-series `series = "A","B"` then
  `"label" = v1,v2`.
- **Common properties:** `title`, `description`, `source`, `sourceUrl`, `byline`, `note`,
  `colors`, `colorPalette`, `sort`, `valueLabels`, `tooltips`.
- **Emphasis:** `highlight "<label>"`; `annotation "<label>" { text; showArrow; anchorDirection;
  textOffsetX; textOffsetY; lineStyle (direct|curve-left|curve-right|elbow); maxWidth }`.
- **Scenes:** `scene "<name>" { title; highlight; data { ... } }` for an animated story.

## Canonical example

```bpc
chart bar-horizontal {
  title = "China emits more CO₂ than the US and India combined"
  description = "Annual emissions in billion tonnes, 2023"
  source = "Our World in Data"
  highlight "China"

  data {
    "China" = 12.17
    "United States" = 4.92
    "India" = 3.06
    "Russia" = 1.73
  }
}
```

## Rules

- Emit `.bpc`, not JSON, Vega-Lite, or matplotlib, unless asked.
- Write the `title` as the lede (the finding in a sentence), not a label.
- Use real fields only; validate before presenting; never present invented data as real.
